  <!-- Frequently Asked Questions -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-5">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How many garage lights do I need for a 20x20 space?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">For a standard 20x20 garage (400 sq ft), you need 6 to 9 recessed lights. For basic parking and storage (20 foot-candles), 6 recessed lights (1,000 lumens each) configured in a 2×3 grid provide clean, shadow-free light. For a workshop, detailing, or gym setup (30 to 50 foot-candles), install 9 recessed lights in a 3×3 grid spaced 6.7 feet apart with a 3.3-foot wall setback.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">What is the best grid spacing for recessed lights in a 20x20 garage?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">For a 3×3 grid (9 lights) in a 20x20 garage, space fixtures 6 feet 8 inches (6.67 ft) apart from center to center. Set the perimeter rows exactly half that distance (3 feet 4 inches) from each wall. This geometry keeps lights safely inside the garage door track rails and illuminates both side walking corridors and the central vehicle aisle.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">What is the difference between lumens and foot-candles?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Lumens measure the total light a fixture produces, while foot-candles measure how much of that light actually hits a surface. Foot-candles are the planning target (IES standards recommend 20 FC for parking, 50 FC for workshops), and fixture lumens are the variable you adjust to meet it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Should I use 4-inch or 6-inch recessed fixtures in a garage?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">For a garage, 6-inch fixtures are highly recommended. They provide a wider beam spread (110°–120°) and higher lumen outputs (1,000–1,200 lm), which are essential for properly illuminating large spaces without harsh shadows.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Do garage door openers affect light placement?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Yes. You must account for the garage door tracks, the opener motor, and the open door itself. Position your rows between the tracks and the side walls, and keep center fixtures clear of the door travel path so the floor remains illuminated when the door is open.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How many LED recessed lights can I put on one 15-amp breaker?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Under NEC Article 210, a standard 15-amp 120V residential circuit supports up to 1,440 watts continuously (80% rule). Modern 6-inch 1,000-lumen LED wafer fixtures consume only 12 to 15 watts each. You could place over 90 LED wafer lights on a single 15-amp circuit without exceeding code limits.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

